 to what we need to do another  way to think about this is the like how  convoluted do we need to make the  evaluation before we can get something  meaningful out of it think of the  question answering tasks that we're in  order for us to use that kind of an  approach we would actually need to have  a possibly a secondary task that we  solve before we can solve the actual  terms that's a very convoluted way of  thinking about this how well can we  identify isolate and fix errors any  model that we do produce is going to  have some errors and we need to be able  to well identify isolate and fix them  ideally so if we have one huge model  that solves the entire problem in one go  any change that we make to that model is  very likely to introduce some  regressions somewhere else  that we didn't intend so thus far we've  kind of gone with with trying to pick  apart the models and and rather apply a  sequence of models then then try to  encapsulate everything into one single  model the problem with that is that the  errors between the models tend to  multiply so if we make an error very  very early in the pipeline then  everything else is somewhat meaningless  there's of course deployment costs we  need to be aware of what the memory  requirements are we need to be aware of  like do we need specialized hardware and  then is there a reliable implementation  available a lot of research nowadays in  NLP tends to have some implementations  available some of them are quite wonky  and you really don't want to use them  some  them are not maybe there's others that I  forgot to mention this is what then sort  of looking at what we currently have in  production so we have kind of two main  tasks or two main levels of looking at  this problem document or document level  classification we have three classifiers  there there's a booking classifier does  this message contain a booking or does  it not contain a booking is it I don't  know is the customer just wanting to  change their address or a frequent flyer  number or is it a cancellation or do  they actually want to book a trip what  is the content of that booking is it a  flight is the hotel is a combination and  then is it finally is it in scope we're  not even trying to handle bookings that  contain you know 15 different flights  and two trains for 17 different people  that's if we see something like that  that's just passed on to the human  travel agents immediately the model that  we're using is the model from the bag of  tricks for efficient text classification  there's the kind of a cartoonish version  of what it looks like but that's  actually the model this is super simple  it's very easy to to implement it it has  relatively low memory requirements it's  very quick to Train this is any works  really well we've been looking at lots  and lots of way more complicated models  with language models and all kinds of  stuff we've been completely unable to  find anything that there's lots of  things that match the performance of  that in terms of just raw metrics but  they also take a week to train so thus  far we've just been sticking with that  then the information instruction or the  slot filling task we currently have a  really weird model that was empowered  inspired by some keggle competitions in  the past don't pay any attention to that  I've made it extra small so that so you  can't read it  it's a convolutional neural network this  does feature extraction and then like a  recurrent neural network on top of that  which makes absolutely no sense to me  that's gonna go away we have this the  model from the from the researchers at  solando we have already tested that I'm  currently training a full sort of  production version of that model we know  that we get a lot lot better performance  out of it so this is absolute  improvement of about 13 percentage  points in f1 averaged over 71 labels  that's of at least for us that's a very  very significant improvement in  performance the only downside is that it  takes a week to train it on a GPU  machine and then we need a GPU machine  to deploy it and so on but you know it  does much much better there's a bunch of  models or there's a bunch of problems  that we haven't been able to find  anything in the research field for one  of the main ones is time expressions  these tend to be extremely difficult to  find and interpret correctly so we were  using a solution from Facebook before  but then we built our own which is  called CT parse that's an open source  library if you want to look at it you  can find it on github it works very well  for us but it is targeted for our use  case so if you want to use it for  something minimum requirement is that  all of your time expressions with point  to the future we do not handle the past  no one wants to book a hotel for last  week there's a bunch of ongoing research  that we're looking into a lot of it is  for the document level classification  we're just sort of trying to tie up some  loose ends and figure out if we can't  find anything better than than the fast  text or the  bag of tricks time parsing as I said  there's a bunch of stuff that I'm  looking into in terms of T the sequence  prediction the the slot filling task  mainly there as well as or the top thing  there is side coupling with pre-trained  language models the model from Salander  research is going to go into production  but there's a whole bunch of other stuff  that we still need to look into and make  sure that that does better and yeah time  parsing recommendation is one we are  hiring if you have any interest in NLP  and recommendation systems and really  complicated datasets come talk to me  here's some pitfalls to avoid start with  really really simple models don't don't  take the latest research just take  something that you know is going to work  and establish a baseline if you don't  have a baseline with a really simple  model that you can run on your laptop  you have absolutely no idea how anything  stacks you you don't know what you  should do  state of the art is often it's the way a  cat the academia works is often very  misleading just getting half a  percentage point of an improvement on  the state of the art is often completely  useless for production we don't care  because there's so many different other  considerations that we need to take into  account that that half a percentage  point on some specific task on some  specific data set all of that goes out  the window once once we incorporate  everything else into it one part of that  everything else is that because we're  trying to create this booking if we get  one single slot wrong on a request the  rest of the Requa  doesn't matter if we don't understand  what the destination of the flight is it  doesn't matter if we get the departure  time correctly and this is where the  this is where the the academic  evaluation measures are often very  misleading for us because it they don't  take these sort of I don't know like  hierarchical evaluation measures often  into account they're focused on the  document level or the token level but we  need to kind of pay attention to both at  the same time once you have a baseline  don't don't be afraid to have just  completely crazy ideas it if even if it  doesn't work it's going to further your  understanding of why it doesn't work  what the data looks like and it at least  it's fun to think about some other  things while your model is training  here's some recommended reading I would  actually even recommend that you read it  in this specific order the NLP is  imagenet moment by service T and roller  that's a really really good overview of  this whole idea of training these  language models then generalized time  which models by Lillian Wang is much  much more in-depth view of like how  specifically do these models work each  and every single one of them and then  finally once you've gotten yourself  really excited about how everything's  gonna be great in the future please go  and read NLP is generalization problem  and then sort of come back down to earth  I apologize for being over time for  those who have been thank you for  listening the work isn't done alone  that's the rest of the team we are a  very fairly small bunch at least for the  time being
 

